<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Ewart Gladstone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Ewart Gladstone was a British Liberal statesman who served four times as prime minister between 1868 and 1894. His governments introduced the secret ballot, state elementary education, army reform and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/representation-of-the-people-act-1884">Third Reform Act</a>, disestablished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a>, and in 1886 and 1893 tried without success to pass Home Rule for Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone.jpg" alt="William Ewart Gladstone" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Alexander Walker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Ewart_Gladstone,_1892_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gladstone was born on 29 December 1809 at 62 Rodney Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, the fourth son of Sir John Gladstone, a wealthy Scottish merchant, slave owner, planter and Tory politician. He was named after his father's friend William Ewart, another Liverpool merchant. The family name was changed from Gladstones to Gladstone by royal licence in 1835. He studied at Eton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and entered the House of Commons in 1832 as a High Tory, winning a seat at Newark with 887 votes; the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> controlled about a quarter of its small electorate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gladstone served in both of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-peel">Sir Robert Peel</a>'s governments and was President of the Board of Trade from 1843 to 1845. When Peel's government fell in 1846 he followed Peel into the Peelite faction, which merged into the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> in 1859. He was Chancellor of the Exchequer under Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> from 1852 to 1855, and again from 1859 to 1866 under Lord Palmerston and Lord Russell. In October 1862 he said in a speech at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a> and the Confederate leaders had &quot;made a nation&quot;, which caused consternation on both sides of the Atlantic. He became leader of the Liberal Party in 1867. He had earlier been a fierce critic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opium-wars">Opium Wars</a>, calling the trade &quot;infamous and atrocious&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Gladstone's first ministry, from 1868 to 1874, passed the disestablishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> and the introduction of secret voting. Told that he was about to become prime minister while he was felling a tree at Hawarden, he declared: &quot;My mission is to pacify Ireland.&quot; After electoral defeat in 1874 he resigned the party leadership. From 1876 he made a comeback on moral opposition to the Ottoman massacres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, publishing Bulgarian Horrors and the Question of the East on 6 September 1876. His Midlothian campaign of 1879 to 1880 is considered the first modern political campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p>His second ministry, from 1880 to 1885, saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/representation-of-the-people-act-1884">Third Reform Act</a>, an Irish Coercion Act in 1881 and a Second Land Act that gave Irish tenants fair rent, fixity of tenure and free sale. Public outrage at the death of General Gordon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> forced him to resign. His brief 1886 ministry was dominated by a home rule proposal for Ireland that was defeated in the Commons and split the Liberals. In 1893 a second Government of Ireland Bill passed the Commons but was defeated in the House of Lords. He resigned in March 1894, aged 84, the oldest prime minister, and left Parliament in 1895.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Ewart Gladstone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two-party politics developed between Conservatives led by figures such as Benjamin Disraeli and Liberals under <b>William Ewart Gladstone</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Hawarden Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1866" aria-label="Liverpool on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William Ewart Gladstone"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4848 5559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4848 5559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1866">What was here in 1866? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Think, I beseech you, think well, think wisely, think, not for the moment, but for the years that are to come, before you reject this Bill.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on the Irish Home Rule Bill, House of Commons, 1886, closing words &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Irish_Home_Rule_Speech" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This, if I understand it, is one of the golden moments of our history — one of those opportunities which may come and may go, but which rarely return, or, if they return, return at long intervals, and under circumstances which no man can forecast.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on the Irish Home Rule Bill, House of Commons, 1886 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Irish_Home_Rule_Speech" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
